1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,930
Lesson 3.1.

2
00:00:02,310 --> 00:00:05,578
Heat, work and entropy
at the trajectory level.

3
00:00:06,683 --> 00:00:09,904
Classical thermodynamics
talks about averages: heat and

4
00:00:09,904 --> 00:00:13,007
work of a gas with ten to
the twenty-three molecules.

5
00:00:13,387 --> 00:00:17,536
But a pbit, a molecule or a
node of a thermodynamic chip are

6
00:00:17,536 --> 00:00:21,131
small systems, where every
realization is different.

7
00:00:21,511 --> 00:00:25,831
Stochastic thermodynamics,
developed by Sekimoto and Seifert,

8
00:00:25,831 --> 00:00:29,725
defines heat, work and entropy
for a single trajectory.

9
00:00:30,105 --> 00:00:33,393
It is the language for measuring
the energy of a noisy computation.

10
00:00:34,517 --> 00:00:37,950
Imagine a particle in a trap
that we move with our hand.

11
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:42,558
When the hand moves the trap,
the particle's potential

12
00:00:42,558 --> 00:00:46,238
energy changes: that is
work, energy we inject.

13
00:00:46,618 --> 00:00:51,389
When the particle moves on its own
within the trap, pushed by the bath, its

14
00:00:51,389 --> 00:00:55,970
energy changes because it exchanges
energy with the fluid: that is heat.

15
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:59,414
The bookkeeping is the
usual one, but done step

16
00:00:59,414 --> 00:01:02,022
by step along a single noisy trajectory.

17
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:08,274
Formally, with an energy V(x, λ)
controlled by a parameter λ(t),

18
00:01:08,654 --> 00:01:12,747
work is the change in
energy from moving the

19
00:01:12,747 --> 00:01:17,770
parameter with the particle
held still: dW = ∂_λ V dλ.

20
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:22,665
The heat received is the
change in energy from the

21
00:01:22,665 --> 00:01:28,084
particle's motion with the
parameter fixed: dQ = ∂_x V ∘ dx,

22
00:01:28,464 --> 00:01:31,030
with Stratonovich's midpoint rule.

23
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:35,164
Adding up, dV = dW +
dQ: the first law holds

24
00:01:35,164 --> 00:01:38,663
on every trajectory, not just on average.

25
00:01:39,783 --> 00:01:42,996
Let's drag a harmonic
trap at constant speed.

26
00:01:43,376 --> 00:01:47,526
At the top you see the moving trap and
the particle following it with a lag.

27
00:01:47,906 --> 00:01:52,073
Below, three counters along the
same trajectory: accumulated

28
00:01:52,073 --> 00:01:56,100
work, accumulated heat and the
change in potential energy.

29
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:59,138
Work grows on average because the particle

30
00:01:59,138 --> 00:02:01,543
lags behind and the hand must pull it.

31
00:02:01,923 --> 00:02:06,379
Heat is mostly negative: the
particle hands energy to the fluid.

32
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:11,183
And at every instant the change in
energy is exactly the sum of both.

33
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:14,947
Let's repeat the experiment
twenty thousand times.

34
00:02:15,327 --> 00:02:19,939
Work varies from one realization to
another and forms a Gaussian distribution.

35
00:02:20,319 --> 00:02:23,541
Its mean is positive, as the
second law demands, because the

36
00:02:23,541 --> 00:02:26,547
free energy does not change
when the trap is translated.

37
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:30,006
But look at the left
tail: in about fifteen

38
00:02:30,006 --> 00:02:33,228
percent of realizations
the work is negative.

39
00:02:33,608 --> 00:02:36,974
The bath, by chance, pushed
the particle in our favor.

40
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:41,934
That does not violate the second law:
the second law is a statement about

41
00:02:41,934 --> 00:02:45,949
averages, and in small systems
individual violations are common.

42
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:49,673
Entropy is also defined per trajectory.

43
00:02:50,053 --> 00:02:55,567
The system entropy is s = −k_B
ln p(x, t), evaluated where

44
00:02:55,567 --> 00:03:00,320
the particle is; its average
is the Gibbs entropy.

45
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:05,296
The entropy of the medium changes as
minus the heat received divided by T.

46
00:03:05,676 --> 00:03:09,556
The total entropy produced along
a trajectory is the sum of both.

47
00:03:09,936 --> 00:03:14,713
Its average is never negative, but
individual trajectories can reduce it.

48
00:03:15,093 --> 00:03:19,755
The next lesson quantifies exactly
how frequent those reductions are.

49
00:03:20,883 --> 00:03:26,998
With stiffness one, friction one, k_B
T equal to one, speed one and duration

50
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:32,146
three, theory gives a mean work
of three minus one minus e^(−3),

51
00:03:32,526 --> 00:03:37,924
about 2.05, and a variance
twice the mean, 4.1.

52
00:03:38,304 --> 00:03:42,374
The simulation gives 2.04 and 4.09.

53
00:03:42,754 --> 00:03:46,342
The predicted probability
of negative work is

54
00:03:46,342 --> 00:03:49,531
15.6 percent, and the measured one 15.5.

55
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:52,278
This matters for computing.

56
00:03:52,658 --> 00:03:55,490
An individual erasure
can cost less than the

57
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:58,321
Landauer bound; only
the average is bounded.

58
00:03:58,701 --> 00:04:01,384
The energy of an operation on a pbit is a

59
00:04:01,384 --> 00:04:03,937
random variable with mean and variance.

60
00:04:04,317 --> 00:04:07,214
And in lab four you will drag a trap to

61
00:04:07,214 --> 00:04:10,409
estimate free-energy
differences from work.

62
00:04:11,517 --> 00:04:16,349
The first law of lesson 1.1
becomes a per-trajectory identity.

63
00:04:16,729 --> 00:04:20,394
Stochastic entropy uses
the Fokker–Planck density.

64
00:04:20,774 --> 00:04:25,420
And the next lessons will turn the
work distribution into exact laws.

65
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:29,787
Common mistakes: mixing
up the sign conventions.

66
00:04:30,167 --> 00:04:34,517
Using the Itô rule for heat,
when the midpoint rule is needed.

67
00:04:34,897 --> 00:04:37,708
Believing a negative work
violates the second law.

68
00:04:38,088 --> 00:04:40,396
And confusing work with
the change in energy.

69
00:04:41,517 --> 00:04:45,257
In summary: work is the change
in energy from moving the

70
00:04:45,257 --> 00:04:49,065
control, and heat, the change
from the particle's motion.

71
00:04:49,445 --> 00:04:52,209
The first law holds
trajectory by trajectory.

72
00:04:52,589 --> 00:04:56,055
Work fluctuates: its mean
respects the second law,

73
00:04:56,055 --> 00:04:59,105
but individual realizations
can be negative.

74
00:05:00,217 --> 00:05:04,844
To practice: write the work and
heat in a moving harmonic trap.

75
00:05:05,224 --> 00:05:07,620
Explain why heat uses Stratonovich.

76
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,438
Predict what happens
when the speed doubles.

77
00:05:10,818 --> 00:05:13,444
And compute the average
of the stochastic entropy.
